-Status: proposal, 28 October 2026. Based on `origin/main` at `b6fbd94c`. The first Metal bring-up is in progress in this task worktree. The macOS workspace check and dev build succeeded, and the offline demo initialized Metal and produced an inspected screenshot after fixing hidden-window startup. Broader visual comparison and validation remain open. No grid test or release validation has been performed.
+Status: updated 29 October 2026. Initial Metal support is merged into the fork; macOS performance investigation and repair are in progress. The human confirmed texture display and object media playback in the real viewer. Broader visual parity, the real-scene performance target and release validation remain open.
+
+## Performance repair objective (29 October 2026)
+
+The human reports about 10 FPS on the Mac and 120–240 FPS in the Windows viewer at the same location. **30–60 FPS in an offline demo is not acceptance.** The goal is to identify and fix the causes of the Mac regression and reach at least 120 FPS, aiming for the reported 120–240 FPS range, in the human's real scene under comparable camera, physical render resolution, graphics settings, loading state and workload. Record hardware and display differences; if a hardware or presentation constraint prevents this, measure and explain it rather than declaring success. Only the human logs in or tests on the grid.
+
+The offline 600-object Metal demo on Apple M2 Pro at 2000×1124 physical pixels produced a median 60 FPS in both development and optimized release builds, 12 loaded one-second samples each. At 2560×1440 the development build produced 30 FPS (44 samples), with 2,136 reported draws in both; the median surface-acquire wait was 16.38 / 31.79 ms. At 2000×1124 the opt-in fixed-count GPU experiment produced 30.05 FPS (24 samples) and increased median CPU `r_finish` from 0.52 to 5.54 ms. A 60 Hz display and observed pacing limit these comparisons; GPU duration was not available in the per-element report. These observations identify bottleneck candidates, not the cause of the real scene's 10 FPS. The earlier attribution of the 30 FPS measurements to the application's background cap was unsupported: timed profiling bypasses that cap.
+
+A follow-up with total GPU timestamps exposed used the same 600-object demo, dev profile, FXAA, no shadows, Immediate presentation and 60 Hz display. After loading, the 2000×1124 run had 16 one-second samples: median 60.0 FPS, 15.84 ms GPU interval, 15.26 ms acquisition wait and 0.52 ms `r_finish`. At 2560×1440, 18 samples gave 30.0 FPS, 24.15 ms GPU interval, 28.94 ms acquisition and 1.53 ms `r_finish`; both recorded 2,136 draws. The GPU interval spans first to last timed pass and can include scheduling gaps or other GPU activity, so it is not a utilization measurement. It rises with resolution in this demo, but the individual pass costs and the user's real-scene cause remain unknown. Logs and captures are retained in the task worktree's ignored `.scratch/` directory.
+
+An initial pass-boundary experiment at 2000×1124 recorded a median 16.81 ms in the opaque scene pass and 20.21 ms for the first-to-last GPU interval (24 samples), while FPS fell to 30.0. This measurement used extra timestamps and is not directly comparable to the earlier 60 FPS run; timing overhead and run-to-run changes have not been isolated. The extra scene-pass timestamps are now opt-in for a controlled comparison.
+
+The first GPU timing runs above cannot establish a GPU bottleneck: later experiments revealed that the reported GPU values can remain identical for many seconds, indicating stale readbacks. The apparent medians are retained as observations, not valid per-frame statistics. A further 2000×1124 comparison with scene-pass timing off and on recorded a median 60 FPS in each run (15 one-second samples each), but the GPU values in the second run froze at 15.53 ms total and 11.61 ms for the scene pass. GPU sample freshness and readback failures must be diagnosed before using these numbers to guide a performance fix.
+
+1. Make valid Metal profiling available: distinguish unavailable GPU measurements from zero, measure total GPU time and surface-acquire / presentation waits, actual physical resolution, draw counts, CPU and frame-time distribution without blocking each frame. Establish stable foreground and release-mode baselines, recording display refresh and present mode.
+2. Reproduce performance bottlenecks in offline representative scenes and isolate output resolution, GPU workload, CPU draw submission, asset streaming and presentation pacing through controlled comparisons. Compare the same scenes and build profile on Windows when hardware is available. Keep observed results, hypotheses and unavailable measurements separate.
+3. Implement and measure targeted fixes. In particular, investigate Metal's individual indirect draw calls and GPU-driven alternatives; do not enable the current fixed-count experiment unless its measured cost and visual behaviour justify it. Preserve graphics fidelity and do not introduce per-frame GPU waits.
+4. Run the relevant automated checks and before/after demo scenarios, then provide a build to the human for the real scene. Completion requires the human's confirmation of the FPS target, with settings and measurement conditions recorded. Keep this task open if only a demo improves or real-world FPS is not measured.
